WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:27.680
Visitors to the Saratoga National Historical
Park in New York may see the Boot Monument,

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:34.070
a frieze that commemorates the Battle of Saratoga,
one of the most significant wins for the Americans

00:00:34.070 --> 00:00:36.910
over the British during the Revolutionary
War.

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:41.920
Specifically, it honors one officer described
as the “most brilliant soldier of the Continental

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:42.920
Army”.

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:48.800
And although it is extremely bizarre, it does
not show his likeness or even name the officer

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:49.800
in question.

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:50.800
Image: Boot Monument
There is a reason for that.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:55.610
The monument is dedicated to Benedict Arnold,
a man presented in history classes around

00:00:55.610 --> 00:01:00.360
the United States as the most notorious traitor
in the country’s history.

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:06.090
There is no better exemplification of the
conundrum posed by Arnold than the Boot Monument.

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:10.220
Before he betrayed his country, he had several
victories that were instrumental to the overall

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:12.290
success of the Continental Army.

00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:16.770
However, constant arguments with his peers
and being the target of numerous slights and

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:22.030
insults, both real and perceived, eventually
persuaded him to switch sides and attempt

00:01:22.030 --> 00:01:24.770
to surrender West Point to the British.

00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:30.270
That’s how we ended up with strange things
like a memorial that does not name its honoree.

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:34.771
People wanted to praise Arnold’s early career
but, at the same time, the mere mention of

00:01:34.771 --> 00:01:37.460
his name revulsed many Americans.

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:42.110
And no, in case you were wondering, the monument
was not erected before Arnold’s betrayal.

00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:47.430
In fact, it was built over a hundred years
later, at the behest of John Watts de Peyster,

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:50.380
himself a Major General during the Civil War.

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:55.510
There is a certain “Jekyll and Hyde” element
to Arnold’s legacy and today we are going

00:01:55.510 --> 00:02:02.520
to try to unravel, as best we can, the tale
of the unnamed hero and the despicable villain

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:05.409
that was Benedict Arnold.

00:02:05.409 --> 00:02:14.459
Early Life and Education
Benedict Arnold was born on January 14, 1741,

00:02:14.459 --> 00:02:17.540
in Norwich, then a part of the Colony of Connecticut.

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:22.670
The second of six children, his parents were
Benedict Arnold Sr. and Hannah Waterman King.

00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:27.560
In fact, the younger Arnold was the fourth
Benedict in his family, all named after his

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:33.209
great-grandfather who served as the Governor
of the Colony of Rhode Island and Providence

00:02:33.209 --> 00:02:34.209
Plantations.

00:02:34.209 --> 00:02:39.010
On his mother’s side, Arnold was descended
from Reverend John Lothropp (sometimes Lathrop),

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:44.160
an influential New England clergyman who helped
settle the city of Barnstable, Massachusetts,

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:49.260
and was one of the first people in the colonies
to champion the idea of separation of church

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:50.260
and state.

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:53.650
At first, it seemed like Arnold would have
an easy ride through life.

00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:57.730
He was born into a wealthy family and his
father was a successful businessman.

00:02:57.730 --> 00:03:02.980
He went to private school in Canterbury, Connecticut,
and was expected to continue his studies at

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:05.550
a prestigious university, most likely Yale.

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:09.750
However, when he was a teenager, three of
his siblings died of Yellow Fever.

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:13.599
His father did not take their losses well
and started drinking heavily.

00:03:13.599 --> 00:03:18.530
In turn, this led to multiple failed business
deals until, eventually, he lost the family

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:19.530
company.

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:22.709
With the money gone, the Arnolds had no choice
but to pull young Benedict from school and

00:03:22.709 --> 00:03:23.709
find him work.

00:03:23.709 --> 00:03:28.270
His mother appealed to two of her cousins,
Daniel and Joshua Lathrop, to take him on

00:03:28.270 --> 00:03:32.710
as an apprentice to their expanding apothecary
business so he could learn the trade.

00:03:32.710 --> 00:03:37.989
She died a few years later, in 1759, followed
by her husband two years after that.

00:03:37.989 --> 00:03:40.380
Arnold proved to be an adept merchant.

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:44.160
Once his apprenticeship was finished, he borrowed
money from his uncles and traveled to Europe

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:45.270
to secure goods.

00:03:45.270 --> 00:03:48.900
He opened his own apothecary in New Haven
which proved to be successful.

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:52.170
In just a few years, he managed to settle
all his family debts.

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:57.680
He also married Margaret Mansfield in 1767
and had three children together before her

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:00.640
death in 1775.

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:08.180
Early Military Career
Arnold’s military career kicked off almost

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:11.060
two decades before the Revolutionary War.

00:04:11.060 --> 00:04:14.470
As a youth, he wanted to fight in the French
and Indian War against his mother’s wishes.

00:04:14.470 --> 00:04:18.849
Eventually, he managed to join a Connecticut
militia but, as far as we can tell, never

00:04:18.849 --> 00:04:20.310
actually saw combat.

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:25.009
As a successful businessman, Arnold felt the
oppressive boot of the British government

00:04:25.009 --> 00:04:26.020
more and more.

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:33.139
The Sugar and Stamp Acts were passed in 1764
and 1765, respectively, to tax the North American

00:04:33.139 --> 00:04:34.139
colonies.

00:04:34.139 --> 00:04:37.460
This was an attempt to increase revenue by
a government that was hurting financially

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:43.720
following the Seven Years’ War, but, unsurprisingly,
proved to be wildly unpopular in North America.

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:48.300
Response to the new taxes was almost universally
negative, but to varying degrees.

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:52.530
Some tried to take the legal route and argued
that it was unconstitutional for the government

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:56.930
to levy direct taxes since only their representative
assemblies could do this.

00:04:56.930 --> 00:05:00.650
“No taxation without representation” became
their rallying slogan.

00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:05.570
Others resorted to mob violence to deter collectors
from doing their jobs.

00:05:05.570 --> 00:05:10.610
Meanwhile, Benedict Arnold and many like him
simply continued to operate normally, in defiance

00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:13.949
of the British Government which, technically,
turned him into a smuggler.

00:05:13.949 --> 00:05:18.370
He also joined the Sons of Liberty, a secret
organization that sought to protect the interests

00:05:18.370 --> 00:05:21.540
of colonists against the British Crown.

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:28.680
Outbreak of the War
Arnold’s first position during the Revolutionary

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:33.700
War was as a captain with a New Haven militia
called the Second Company of Governor’s

00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:34.700
Foot Guard.

00:05:34.700 --> 00:05:39.380
When the Battles at Lexington and Concord
signaled the start of the conflict, his men

00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:40.759
were ready to march.

00:05:40.759 --> 00:05:42.840
Well...maybe not so fast.

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:48.340
The militia was ready to go, but the New Haven
town council led by David Wooster had a meeting

00:05:48.340 --> 00:05:51.410
and decided that they should wait for regular
orders.

00:05:51.410 --> 00:05:55.110
Consequently, they refused to release any
gunpowder to the soldiers.

00:05:55.110 --> 00:06:00.270
Back then, because gunpowder was so volatile
and dangerous, a town’s entire supply was

00:06:00.270 --> 00:06:05.990
generally kept in one safe place - usually,
a magazine or a church basement - and the

00:06:05.990 --> 00:06:08.270
keys were safeguarded carefully.

00:06:08.270 --> 00:06:13.180
Frustrated by the denial, Arnold confronted
Wooster in a tavern and demanded the keys,

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:17.090
saying that he intended to march even if he
had to break into the powder house.

00:06:17.090 --> 00:06:21.009
The town council relented and, after arming
themselves, the militia trooped to Cambridge,

00:06:21.009 --> 00:06:22.009
Massachusetts.

00:06:22.009 --> 00:06:24.480
Image suggestion: Powder House Day celebration
This little moment of glory for Benedict Arnold

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:26.360
is well-remembered in New Haven.

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:32.300
Since 1904, the city commemorates it every
year on April 22 as Powder House Day.

00:06:32.300 --> 00:06:39.740
On the Way to Fort Ticonderoga
Arnold’s militia was on its way to take

00:06:39.740 --> 00:06:45.289
part in the Siege of Boston, but on the way
met Colonel Samuel Holden Parsons.

00:06:45.289 --> 00:06:49.110
The two of them discussed the Continental
Army’s desperate need for artillery and

00:06:49.110 --> 00:06:53.300
brought up the fact that Fort Ticonderoga
on Lake Champlain had plenty of cannons guarded

00:06:53.300 --> 00:06:55.050
only by a small force.

00:06:55.050 --> 00:06:59.570
They agreed that the fort would be a great
asset to them so, while Parsons continued

00:06:59.570 --> 00:07:03.520
his journey, Arnold went to Cambridge where
he persuaded the Massachusetts Committee of

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:06.330
Safety to fund his expedition to capture the
fort.

00:07:06.330 --> 00:07:08.680
Image: Ethan Allen
Now we arrive at the first instance where

00:07:08.680 --> 00:07:11.389
Arnold felt slighted by his own side.

00:07:11.389 --> 00:07:14.699
He was not the only one planning to attack
Fort Ticonderoga.

00:07:14.699 --> 00:07:19.479
A second militia from the New Hampshire Grants
called the Green Mountain Boys were also on

00:07:19.479 --> 00:07:20.479
route.

00:07:20.479 --> 00:07:25.240
They were led by Ethan Allen, a man who would
go on to be one of the founders of Vermont.

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:29.930
Arnold and Allen did not get along and this
was the first in a series of rivalries that

00:07:29.930 --> 00:07:32.710
Benedict would have with fellow officers.

00:07:32.710 --> 00:07:37.169
Whether or not he was justified in his enmity,
we cannot really say with certainty since

00:07:37.169 --> 00:07:40.419
both sides gave their own versions of the
story.

00:07:40.419 --> 00:07:44.919
Benedict Arnold had been made a colonel and
had official orders to lead the charge on

00:07:44.919 --> 00:07:46.540
Fort Ticonderoga.

00:07:46.540 --> 00:07:51.259
This did not mean anything to Allen who refused
to relinquish command nor to his Green Mountain

00:07:51.259 --> 00:07:54.190
Boys who would not obey the orders of any
other officer.

00:07:54.190 --> 00:07:58.360
They knew they had all the leverage because
they accounted for the bulk of the military

00:07:58.360 --> 00:07:59.360
force.

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:03.630
Eventually, Arnold had no choice but to concede
and accept Ethan Allen as the co-leader of

00:08:03.630 --> 00:08:04.640
the expedition.

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:07.440
Some historians see it as even more insulting
to Arnold.

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:11.949
They argue that he was only allowed to ride
at the front of the military column alongside

00:08:11.949 --> 00:08:15.130
Allen for pure symbolism and had no actual
authority.

00:08:15.130 --> 00:08:20.389
We should mention that there was also a third,
smaller force led by one Colonel James Easton

00:08:20.389 --> 00:08:25.479
who got along even worse with Arnold and their
relationship eventually descended into violence.

00:08:25.479 --> 00:08:29.860
According to Arnold’s journal, he took the
liberty of “breaking his head” and then

00:08:29.860 --> 00:08:31.850
challenging Easton to a duel.

00:08:31.850 --> 00:08:36.130
When the latter refused to relinquish his
other weapons and “draw like a gentleman”,

00:08:36.130 --> 00:10:04.199
Arnold kicked him “very heartily” and
told him to leave

00:10:04.199 --> 00:10:05.679
the point.

00:10:05.679 --> 00:10:18.269
Taking the

00:10:18.269 --> 00:10:42.049
Fort

00:10:42.049 --> 00:10:46.709
Despite the animosity between Arnold and his
fellow officers, the attack on Fort Ticonderoga

00:10:46.709 --> 00:10:48.930
was an unmitigated success.

00:10:48.930 --> 00:10:54.610
During the early hours of May 10, 1775, around
80 soldiers crossed Lake Champlain undetected

00:10:54.610 --> 00:10:57.959
and snuck into the fort, taking the British
garrison by surprise.

00:10:57.959 --> 00:11:01.389
The militia managed to capture the citadel
without any fatalities.

00:11:01.389 --> 00:11:06.491
From there, the men also took the nearby Fort
George and Fort Crown Point which had even

00:11:06.491 --> 00:11:07.990
fewer soldiers guarding them.

00:11:07.990 --> 00:11:09.870
This victory was cause for celebration.

00:11:09.870 --> 00:11:13.819
According to Arnold’s journal, the Green
Mountain Boys broke into the rum supply and

00:11:13.819 --> 00:11:15.499
drank themselves into a stupor.

00:11:15.499 --> 00:11:18.819
Meanwhile, his own captains started to show
up with additional men.

00:11:18.819 --> 00:11:24.019
Now that he had a sizable force on his side,
Arnold sought to claim some glory for himself.

00:11:24.019 --> 00:11:27.809
His target was Fort Saint-Jean on the Canadian
side of Lake Champlain.

00:11:27.809 --> 00:11:32.579
Like the other fortresses, it was poorly guarded,
but it also had the HMS Royal George, the

00:11:32.579 --> 00:11:34.589
only major warship in those waters.

00:11:34.589 --> 00:11:38.740
It would have been a fitting prize for the
ambitious officer who knew that Ethan Allen

00:11:38.740 --> 00:11:42.660
would try to claim as much credit as possible
for the capture of Fort Ticonderoga.

00:11:42.660 --> 00:11:47.370
Indeed, when he wrote his memoir, Allen neglected
to mention Benedict Arnold by name.

00:11:47.370 --> 00:11:51.439
A week after taking Ticonderoga, Arnold’s
men rowed in small boats called bateaux and

00:11:51.439 --> 00:11:52.709
reached Fort Saint-Jean undetected.

00:11:52.709 --> 00:11:58.429
The element of surprise was enough to overpower
the small garrison, but Arnold found out that

00:11:58.429 --> 00:12:00.929
a large British force was on its way from
Montreal.

00:12:00.929 --> 00:12:03.199
The capture of the fort turned into a raid.

00:12:03.199 --> 00:12:07.350
Arnold and his men took all the supplies they
could aboard the Royal George and sunk everything

00:12:07.350 --> 00:12:08.350
else.

00:12:08.350 --> 00:12:09.911
Image: USS Enterprise
Arnold decided to rechristen the warship as

00:12:09.911 --> 00:12:11.509
the USS Enterprise.

00:12:11.509 --> 00:12:15.910
It became the first in a long line of prestigious
American ships that carried that name - a

00:12:15.910 --> 00:12:21.009
tradition which, if Star Trek is to be believed,
will continue hundreds of years into the future.

00:12:21.009 --> 00:12:26.339
Ethan Allen and his men started to disperse
from Fort Ticonderoga and Arnold assumed that

00:12:26.339 --> 00:12:27.499
he would take command.

00:12:27.499 --> 00:12:32.040
Instead, Colonel Benjamin Hinman arrived with
a large force of 1,000 soldiers and orders

00:12:32.040 --> 00:12:36.660
from Connecticut Governor Jonathan Trumbull
which said that Arnold would serve as his

00:12:36.660 --> 00:12:37.660
subordinate.

00:12:37.660 --> 00:12:41.790
Incensed by another perceived injustice, Arnold
resigned his commission and headed home.

00:12:41.790 --> 00:12:46.209
On the way, he learned that his wife had passed
away and, shortly after, Arnold himself was

00:12:46.209 --> 00:12:51.799
struck by gout, a painful disease which would
plague him for the rest of his life.

00:12:51.799 --> 00:12:58.999
Disaster in Canada
Despite these setbacks, Arnold was not quite

00:12:58.999 --> 00:13:02.180
ready to give up on the military life just
yet.

00:13:02.180 --> 00:13:06.109
Among others, he was of the opinion that the
Continental Army’s next move should be to

00:13:06.109 --> 00:13:10.730
invade Quebec via their newly-gained foothold
on Lake Champlain.

00:13:10.730 --> 00:13:14.749
This would deny the British a solid base of
operations should they want to charge into

00:13:14.749 --> 00:13:15.749
New York.

00:13:15.749 --> 00:13:19.769
Again, Arnold was refused the opportunity
to lead the expedition, with that honor going

00:13:19.769 --> 00:13:21.190
to Major General Philip Schuyler.

00:13:21.190 --> 00:13:25.059
He turned out to be quite a rarity in that
he was a Continental officer that Benedict

00:13:25.059 --> 00:13:26.889
Arnold actually got along with.

00:13:26.889 --> 00:13:30.740
However, he was stricken by illness and was
forced to relinquish command to Brigadier

00:13:30.740 --> 00:13:31.740
General Richard Montgomery.

00:13:31.740 --> 00:13:34.629
Image: Richard Montgomery
Arnold convinced his superiors that a two-pronged

00:13:34.629 --> 00:13:36.100
attack would work better.

00:13:36.100 --> 00:13:41.109
He was given 1,100 men and a colonel’s commission
by George Washington himself and told to meet

00:13:41.109 --> 00:13:43.779
up with Montgomery’s forces and take Quebec
City.

00:13:43.779 --> 00:13:45.579
There was just one problem.

00:13:45.579 --> 00:13:49.499
In order for this assault to work, Arnold
had to take his troops through the harsh wilderness

00:13:49.499 --> 00:13:54.239
of Maine and Quebec, over rough terrain, in
cold September weather.

00:13:54.239 --> 00:13:57.519
Almost half of his soldiers died or deserted
before they reached their goal.

00:13:57.519 --> 00:14:01.470
Meanwhile, the British got wind of this planned
attack and shored up their defenses.

00:14:01.470 --> 00:14:05.239
The six hundred or so soldiers who remained
joined up with an equal force commanded by

00:14:05.239 --> 00:14:10.509
General Montgomery and, on December 31, 1775,
attacked Quebec City.

00:14:10.509 --> 00:14:13.540
The battle was a disaster for the continental
forces.

00:14:13.540 --> 00:14:15.459
Montgomery died leading the assault.

00:14:15.459 --> 00:14:18.190
Most of his soldiers were killed, injured,
or taken prisoner.

00:14:18.190 --> 00:14:22.980
The remaining forces fell under the command
of Benedict Arnold who sustained a leg injury

00:14:22.980 --> 00:14:24.269
during the fight.

00:14:24.269 --> 00:14:29.239
Instead of retreating, Arnold decided to maintain
a siege on the city in the hopes that reinforcements

00:14:29.239 --> 00:14:30.239
would come.

00:14:30.239 --> 00:14:34.980
New units did arrive, commanded by David Wooster,
the same man who didn’t want to supply Arnold’s

00:14:34.980 --> 00:14:35.980
militia with gunpowder.

00:14:35.980 --> 00:14:40.790
He was now a major general and took control
of the siege while Arnold traveled to Montreal.

00:14:40.790 --> 00:14:44.179
As a consolation prize, he found out that
he had been promoted to brigadier general

00:14:44.179 --> 00:14:45.179
for his efforts.

00:14:45.179 --> 00:14:47.279
Legal Troubles
Continental forces gave up on Quebec City

00:14:47.279 --> 00:14:51.660
once British reinforcements were on the way
and it became clear that there was no salvaging

00:14:51.660 --> 00:14:53.439
that disastrous mission.

00:14:53.439 --> 00:14:57.970
Unsurprisingly, the enemy pressed their advantage
and continued on to Montreal to gain back

00:14:57.970 --> 00:15:00.399
the territory they had lost in Canada.

00:15:00.399 --> 00:15:04.199
Arnold spent the rest of the year in Quebec,
trying to slow down the advance of the British

00:15:04.199 --> 00:15:05.379
as much as possible.

00:15:05.379 --> 00:15:09.759
He distinguished himself in two conflicts:
the Battle of the Cedars and the Battle of

00:15:09.759 --> 00:15:10.959
Valcour Island.

00:15:10.959 --> 00:15:14.939
However, because it had been a while since
he had an argument with another officer, Arnold

00:15:14.939 --> 00:15:19.119
brought charges of dereliction of duty against
General Moses Hazen.

00:15:19.119 --> 00:15:23.389
He claimed that the latter refused to take
goods from Montreal merchants and send them

00:15:23.389 --> 00:15:25.419
to supply Arnold’s garrison.

00:15:25.419 --> 00:15:29.720
This led to a court-martial for Hazen, but
his rank, his service, and his connections

00:15:29.720 --> 00:15:31.160
ensured that he got off.

00:15:31.160 --> 00:15:35.209
In turn, he pressed counter charges against
Arnold and had him court-martialed.

00:15:35.209 --> 00:15:39.889
Moreover, he was joined by two junior officers
who had their own issues with Arnold, John

00:15:39.889 --> 00:15:42.819
Brown and the aforementioned James Easton.

00:15:42.819 --> 00:15:47.370
Unlike Hazen, Arnold was never honorably acquitted
of the accusations against him.

00:15:47.370 --> 00:15:51.259
Instead, General Horatio Gates intervened
and dissolved the arrest warrant, believing

00:15:51.259 --> 00:15:55.100
that the brigadier general was too valuable
in battle to lose over legal matters.

00:15:55.100 --> 00:16:00.019
It certainly sounds like Gates was on the
same page as Arnold, but stick around because

00:16:00.019 --> 00:16:02.540
that will not last long.

00:16:02.540 --> 00:16:07.189
Meanwhile, in a rather prophetic move, John
Brown was not content with the ruling in Arnold’s

00:16:07.189 --> 00:16:08.189
favor.

00:16:08.189 --> 00:16:11.730
He wanted the world to know what kind of man
the general was and distributed a pamphlet

00:16:11.730 --> 00:16:16.419
where he wrote of Benedict Arnold that “money
is this man’s God, and to get enough of

00:16:16.419 --> 00:16:20.220
it he would sacrifice his country”.

00:16:20.220 --> 00:16:27.410
Success at Saratoga
We are approaching the crowning achievement

00:16:27.410 --> 00:16:31.680
of Benedict Arnold’s military career - his
service during the Battles of Saratoga.

00:16:31.680 --> 00:16:36.529
However, it almost never happened because,
once again, he wanted to resign his position.

00:16:36.529 --> 00:16:40.619
Arnold was angered by the fact that several
junior officers, some of them his enemies,

00:16:40.619 --> 00:16:42.699
were promoted to major general ahead of him.

00:16:42.699 --> 00:16:47.309
It was only due to George Washington’s direct
interference that he decided to postpone his

00:16:47.309 --> 00:16:51.689
resignation and, instead, serve under General
Schuyler at Saratoga.

00:16:51.689 --> 00:16:56.119
Eventually, Continental Congress promoted
Arnold to major general, but refused to reinstate

00:16:56.119 --> 00:17:00.540
his seniority, meaning that those junior officers
still remained his superiors.

00:17:00.540 --> 00:17:05.790
At Saratoga, Arnold found out not only of
Congress’ refusal to restore his seniority,

00:17:05.790 --> 00:17:10.230
but also that Schuyler had been replaced with
General Horatio Gates.

00:17:10.230 --> 00:17:14.160
The general might have defended Arnold during
his court-martial, but their relationship

00:17:14.160 --> 00:17:18.540
soured quickly on the battlefield as they
had opposing views on how to approach the

00:17:18.540 --> 00:17:19.819
military campaign.

00:17:19.819 --> 00:17:24.800
The first engagement occurred on September
19 against British forces led by General John

00:17:24.800 --> 00:17:25.800
Burgoyne.

00:17:25.800 --> 00:17:29.470
Dubbed the Battle of Freeman’s Farm, it
was a pyrrhic victory for the British.

00:17:29.470 --> 00:17:33.610
They might have won the field, but they suffered
greater casualties and failed to weaken the

00:17:33.610 --> 00:17:34.610
American position.

00:17:34.610 --> 00:17:36.950
Image: John Burgoyne
Most of the success was due to Arnold who

00:17:36.950 --> 00:17:40.419
wanted to be aggressive and took his regiments
to meet the British head-on.

00:17:40.419 --> 00:17:44.860
However, he felt that he didn’t receive
proper credit in the reports written by Gates’s

00:17:44.860 --> 00:17:48.259
party and began butting heads with the superior
officer.

00:17:48.259 --> 00:17:53.049
One shouting match got so heated that Gates
relieved Arnold of command although, for an

00:17:53.049 --> 00:17:56.049
unclear reason, the latter remained in camp.

00:17:56.049 --> 00:18:01.320
This proved instrumental in the second clash
at Saratoga known as the Battle of Bemis Heights.

00:18:01.320 --> 00:18:06.210
According to the conventional story, Arnold
ignored Gates’s orders and charged into

00:18:06.210 --> 00:18:10.930
battle at a critical point, rallied the men
and captured a British redoubt.

00:18:10.930 --> 00:18:15.039
It was also at this point that Arnold was
first shot in the leg and then had it crushed

00:18:15.039 --> 00:18:16.840
by his horse falling on it.

00:18:16.840 --> 00:18:21.110
A newly-discovered letter suggests that Arnold
had his superior’s permission to engage,

00:18:21.110 --> 00:18:23.570
but this is besides the point.

00:18:23.570 --> 00:18:27.929
What matters is that Benedict Arnold ensured
a victory for the Continental Army that played

00:18:27.929 --> 00:18:31.169
a pivotal role in the outcome of the Revolutionary
War.

00:18:31.169 --> 00:18:36.130
A few days later, General Burgoyne surrendered
and this was the move that convinced France

00:18:36.130 --> 00:18:40.080
to enter the war as an ally against the British.

00:18:40.080 --> 00:18:47.460
In Charge of Philadelphia
Following the Battles of Saratoga, Arnold

00:18:47.460 --> 00:18:50.070
took time off active duty to heal his injuries.

00:18:50.070 --> 00:18:54.659
In June 1778, after the British withdrew from
Philadelphia, Washington placed him in command

00:18:54.659 --> 00:18:55.659
of the city.

00:18:55.659 --> 00:18:57.940
Image: Peggy Shippen
His new position provided Arnold ample opportunity

00:18:57.940 --> 00:19:02.480
to enrich himself through various shady business
deals and questionable schemes.

00:19:02.480 --> 00:19:07.740
This increase in status allowed the major
general to meet the Shippens, one of the wealthiest,

00:19:07.740 --> 00:19:09.299
most influential families in Philadelphia.

00:19:09.299 --> 00:19:14.750
He fell in love with 18-year-old Peggy Shippen
and the two married in April, 1779.

00:19:14.750 --> 00:19:18.460
It’s important to remember that, at this
point in time, there were still people in

00:19:18.460 --> 00:19:21.350
the colonies who sympathized with the British
government.

00:19:21.350 --> 00:19:22.890
The Shippens were among them.

00:19:22.890 --> 00:19:27.720
Now, it’s impossible to say with certainty
how influential Peggy was in getting her husband

00:19:27.720 --> 00:19:33.149
to switch sides, but she definitely exposed
him to a new circle of individuals who shared

00:19:33.149 --> 00:19:34.250
her views.

00:19:34.250 --> 00:19:37.750
Crucially, among them was British spy chief
Major John André.

00:19:37.750 --> 00:19:43.010
In fact, historians believe that the two of
them had a romantic tryst before Peggy married

00:19:43.010 --> 00:19:44.010
Arnold.

00:19:44.010 --> 00:19:45.010
Image: John Andre
Around the same time, the general had been

00:19:45.010 --> 00:19:47.970
court-martialed for abusing his power in Philadelphia.

00:19:47.970 --> 00:19:52.330
He was found guilty on two charges of using
government transport for personal use and

00:19:52.330 --> 00:19:55.529
issuing a pass for a ship he had a financial
stake in.

00:19:55.529 --> 00:19:59.790
Arnold received a public chastising from George
Washington who wrote that his sense of duty

00:19:59.790 --> 00:20:04.289
compelled him to decry the general’s actions
as “imprudent and improper”.

00:20:04.289 --> 00:20:08.190
Whether this was the straw that broke the
camel’s back or he had already made up his

00:20:08.190 --> 00:20:10.580
mind by this point, we don’t know.

00:20:10.580 --> 00:20:14.960
But Arnold had decided to betray his country.

00:20:14.960 --> 00:20:22.230
Betrayal at West Point
Major André acted as a middle-man between

00:20:22.230 --> 00:20:26.660
Benedict Arnold and Henry Clinton, the British
Commander-in-Chief in North America.

00:20:26.660 --> 00:20:29.679
The offer was £20,000 and a general’s commission.

00:20:29.679 --> 00:20:34.389
In return, Arnold would surrender West Point,
a military post on the Hudson River with significant

00:20:34.389 --> 00:20:35.389
strategic value.

00:20:35.389 --> 00:20:38.240
Image: West Point
On August 3, 1780, Arnold assumed command

00:20:38.240 --> 00:20:42.759
and began weakening the post’s defenses
by delaying repairs, draining supplies, and

00:20:42.759 --> 00:20:43.950
reassigning troops.

00:20:43.950 --> 00:20:48.059
Communication was slow and dangerous between
him and the British so it wasn’t until late

00:20:48.059 --> 00:20:50.100
September that he met André again.

00:20:50.100 --> 00:20:54.929
The plot was exposed on September 23 when
the spy was caught by militiamen who found

00:20:54.929 --> 00:20:58.169
incriminating papers on him that detailed
the entire plan.

00:20:58.169 --> 00:21:02.690
André was taken into custody, but Arnold
found out of his arrest in time to flee to

00:21:02.690 --> 00:21:05.909
British-controlled New York City aboard the
HMS Vulture.

00:21:05.909 --> 00:21:10.210
Peggy managed to join him and the family moved
to London following the end of the war.

00:21:10.210 --> 00:21:12.769
Major André was hanged as a spy.

00:21:12.769 --> 00:21:20.659
General Explains His Actions
In early October, Arnold published an open

00:21:20.659 --> 00:21:25.620
letter titled “To the Inhabitants of America”
to explain why he did it.

00:21:25.620 --> 00:21:29.580
Unsurprisingly, it wasn’t because of his
constant fights with other officers, or his

00:21:29.580 --> 00:21:32.690
illicit dealings, or his wife’s persuasions.

00:21:32.690 --> 00:21:37.679
It was because of the tyranny and corruption
of Congress and the leaders of the Continental

00:21:37.679 --> 00:21:38.679
Army.

00:21:38.679 --> 00:21:42.390
Arnold argued that once the British government
sorted out the grievances of Americans, there

00:21:42.390 --> 00:21:46.059
was no reason why the colonies should not
return to the British Empire.

00:21:46.059 --> 00:21:49.490
He also objected to America’s alliance with
Catholic France.

00:21:49.490 --> 00:21:56.549
As you might expect, public reception to the
letter was “unfavorable”, to say the least.

00:21:56.549 --> 00:22:00.789
Even though the treasonous plot failed, Arnold
was still rewarded handsomely by the British

00:22:00.789 --> 00:22:01.789
Crown.

00:22:01.789 --> 00:22:06.190
He was granted pay, pensions for his whole
family, and a commission as brigadier general.

00:22:06.190 --> 00:22:10.559
However, he never distinguished himself in
battle for the remainder of the war.

00:22:10.559 --> 00:22:14.289
Arnold’s life after his betrayal was, fittingly,
bereft of any triumphs.

00:22:14.289 --> 00:22:16.750
He made several bad business deals in London
and Saint John, New Brunswick, and had to

00:22:16.750 --> 00:22:18.340
deal with multiple lawsuits.

00:22:18.340 --> 00:22:23.270
His propensity to anger his peers continued
on the other side of the pond and, in 1792,

00:22:23.270 --> 00:22:27.370
Arnold fought a bloodless duel with James
Maitland, the Earl of Lauderdale.

00:22:27.370 --> 00:22:29.960
His gout had gotten worse and affected his
good leg.

00:22:29.960 --> 00:22:35.299
Eventually, he died in a state of delirium
in 1801, aged 60, leaving behind an indebted

00:22:35.299 --> 00:22:36.299
estate.

00:22:36.299 --> 00:22:40.370
In the centuries that followed, “Benedict
Arnold” became synonymous with “traitor”

00:22:40.370 --> 00:22:45.500
in the United States, with very few people
remembering (or caring, for that matter) about

00:22:45.500 --> 00:22:48.059
his service in the war prior to his betrayal.

00:22:48.059 --> 00:22:53.110
The only people still troubled by Arnold are
historians who still struggle, to this day,

00:22:53.110 --> 00:22:58.700
to find the most accurate perspective to look
at one of the country’s most controversial

00:22:58.700 --> 00:23:29.340
figures.

